  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 AM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nAfter a lull for the past couple of days, the monsoon is coming back \nwith a vengeance. Showers and embedded thunder have broken out across \nmuch of western Wyoming as I write this shortly after midnight. Most \nof these should continue until around 3 am before tapering to just a \nfew stragglers for the rest of the overnight like last night. \n\nThings ramp up considerably today, as much deeper monsoonal moisture \nmoves over the area. Precipitable waters, which right now are \nrunning around 150 percent of normal, are now expected to climb as \nhigh as 1.3 inches at out office and as high as 1.5 inches in other \nlocations. This is more than 200 percent of normal for many\nareas. The Weather Prediction Center has also increased the\nslight risk of excessive rainfall over southwestern Wyoming, \nwith a marginal risk across all but the northeastern portion of\nour forecast area. As a result, with some rainfall rates that \ncould be over 2 inches an hour, we will hoist a flash flood \nwatch starting at noon today for all areas across our county \nwarning area. The best chance today would be West of the Divide,\nbut the models, both the synoptic and high resolution, have \nbeen doing a terrible job in showing the placement of the \nconvection. Case and point, showers popped up in Washakie and \nJohnson County and no models had it there. It does look like the\nprogression will start in western Wyoming and spread east \nthrough the day. And like the past few nights, most convection \nshould end by midnight, but some showers and a few storms should\nlinger through into Wednesday morning. As for temperatures, \nwith more cloud cover, temperatures will still be cooler than \nyesterday. Still quite warm, but generally 3 to 8 degrees cooler\nthan Monday.\n\nThe main event still looks to be on Wednesday. Precipitable water \nvalues will reach a zenith on this day, topping out at around 1.5 \ninches at our office (around 250 percent of normal). If this comes \nto fruition, this would be a new record high for the office (the old \nrecord is 1.42 inches). The main difference will be a shortwave that \nwill tracking across the area during peak heating of the day, adding \nsome additional lift. With the combination of the possibly\nrecord moist atmosphere and the passing shortwave, this looks to\nbe most likely day with flooding problems. The Weather\nPrediction Center also agrees and has expanded the slight risk\nof excessive rainfall to cover most of the County Warning Area.\nAs a result, we will continue the flood watch through this day \nas well. Another difference here is that a lot of the convection\nwill linger through the evening and possibly even into the \nnight time hours, much for than the previous couple of nights. \nAnd with this, the flood watch was run though Wednesday night.\n\nWe should begin a downward trend starting on Thursday. Most \nguidance shows the main plume starting to moving to the east \nand south with precipitable water values dropping, especially \nWest of the Divide. All in all, values look to drop between 20 \nand 30 percent. However, this is still close to 200 percent of \nnormal, especially across areas East of the Divide. However, \ncoverage should be less with somewhat less moisture and the lack\nof a trigger like Wednesday's shortwave. There is still the \nrisk of flooding on this day given the remaining moisture and \npossible saturated ground from the previous days convection, \nalthough it should be somewhat less than Tuesday and especially \nWednesday. We will let future shifts make the decision whether \nor not to extend it through Thursday.\n\nFriday is when it finally looks like the main area of moisture will \nretreat out of the area and chances of convection finally go back to \nnormal, giving the people of Wyoming and meteorologists a break \n(including me, I HATE the monsoon!). There could be another \nsecondary surge over the weekend, but it should not be anything like \nit has been during the middle of this week. In addition, the ridge \nwill still be present over the area through the period. So, after a \nfew days of cooler weather with more cloud cover. Well above normal \ntemperatures look likely to return with triple digit high \ntemperatures possible in eastern Big Horn Basin possibly on both \nSaturday and Sunday.",
